{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7YAu2omEHQB2YumqNn9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Some commands can't be undone, and that alone is a reason to run them only while the user is watching. Deleting files, force-pushing over a branch, dropping a database table, or sending an email leaves no undo button. When an agent runs a command like that unattended, the first time anyone sees the result is after the damage is done. A person watching can still stop it, catch a wrong path or wrong branch before Enter, and see a mistake while it is still small. The command costs the same either way. What changes is when a mistake gets noticed, and for these commands noticing early is the only chance to prevent the damage.

Other commands should be watched because they act with the user's identity and authority. Pushing to a shared repository, publishing a package, using credentials, charging a card, or posting a comment all take place in the user's name and reach people who never agreed to deal with an agent. The user answers for those actions, so they should decide on them, and they can't decide on something they never saw. This is also why tools like Ouijit put such commands in front of the user as an exact line to approve or decline instead of running them quietly. Showing the literal command, not a summary of it, leaves the user nothing to guess about what they are approving.

